# RUNBOOK — build the app in `task/` and get it verified

You are working in `/workspace`: a native SwiftUI iOS project (Swift 5.9, iOS 17, one
target `{{APP}}`, no package dependencies — the standard library and SwiftUI are all you
have and all you need), a `task/` directory with the product contract, and this runbook.
This file is environment knowledge only — the task is defined solely by the contract files.

## Your job

Implement the mobile app defined in:
- `task/instruction.md` (root contract: product, exact seed data, formats, rules), and
- `task/steps/01-*/instruction.md` → `task/steps/02-*/instruction.md` → `task/steps/03-*/instruction.md`
  (step contracts, in that order — each builds on the previous).

Your work is graded by a hidden suite of **four device tests** run on a cloud iOS
simulator against a Release build of the source you submit: one test per step (`t_1`,
`t_2`, `t_3`) plus a final end-to-end test (`final`). **Every submission is graded against
all four**, so a later change that breaks an earlier step is caught. The run ends when all
four pass, when you have used all **{{MAX_SUBMISSIONS}} submissions**, or at the wall clock
({{WALL_CLOCK_MIN}} min from start).

**A submission is a final-exam attempt, not a progress check.** Check steps in the
development loop below. Submit only when you have verified on-device that all three steps
AND the full end-to-end journey work. Expect your first submission to be your only one.

## How your run is evaluated

In priority order:
1. **What passes** — the verdict vector of your final submission; completing everything
   dominates all other factors.
2. **How few submissions you used** — each extra submission meaningfully lowers your
   evaluation. Submitting to see what the hidden tests say is a losing strategy.
3. **How little agent time you spent** — time you spend working (build/grading wait is
   excluded). Verifying on-device is time well spent; idling and flailing are not.
Breaking something that previously passed (a regression) is penalized separately and
severely. Protocol violations (see Environment facts) can invalidate the run entirely.

Read `AGENTS.md` first. Commit your work in small, clear commits as you go (`git` is
local-only; there is no remote).

## Checkpoint every step (required — not a submission)

The moment you believe a step is complete — verified on a device, before you start the
next one — record it:

```bash
./checkpoint.sh 1        # after finishing step 1; likewise 2 and 3
```

This commits and tags your tree as that step's boundary. It is NOT a submission: nothing is
graded, no feedback is returned, and it does not consume any budget. It is required even
when everything works first try. `./checkpoint.sh list` shows what you have recorded.

## Environment facts

- Tools on PATH: `git`, `jq`, `python3`, `revyl` (Revyl CLI). **There is no Swift compiler
  in this container**: compilation happens on Revyl's build runner, through `./devbuild.sh`
  (development) and `./submit.sh` (grading). Never run `revyl upgrade`. Never invent CLI
  flags — check `revyl <cmd> --help` first.
- Source lives under `Sources/{{APP}}/`, a synchronized folder: any `.swift` file you add
  anywhere under it is compiled, no project edit needed. **Never hand-edit
  `{{APP}}.xcodeproj`** or `project.yml`.
- No clocks or randomness in app logic (`Date()`, `.random`, `UUID()` shown on screen): the
  contract fixes every value.
- IDs you need are in `infra_runtime.json` (`rollout_id`, `agent_dev_app_id`,
  `max_submissions`, `submissions_used`, `max_dev_builds`, `dev_builds_used`).
- Do not create Revyl apps, builds or tests by hand (`revyl app create`, `revyl build` run
  directly, `revyl test create` are protocol violations); use `./devbuild.sh` and
  `./submit.sh` only. Do not edit `submit.sh`, `devbuild.sh`, `infra_runtime.json`,
  `.revyl/config.yaml`, anything under `task/` or `.devbuilds/`, and never delete
  `.revyl/device-sessions.json`.

## Iterating on a device (a remote build per iteration — there is no hot reload)

Every look at your app on a device costs one remote build of two to four minutes, and your
budget is **{{MAX_DEV_BUILDS}} development builds**. Batch your edits: implement a whole
step, re-read it against the contract, then build.

```bash
./devbuild.sh            # commits your tree, queues a Debug simulator build: BUILD k=<k> job=<job>
./devbuild.sh wait <k>   # polls ≤5 min: READY k=<k> version-id=<id>, or FAILED with the compiler lines, or PENDING (call again)
./devbuild.sh status     # builds used / max, last build state
```
A failed build costs one of the {{MAX_DEV_BUILDS}}: read the `error:` lines it prints (file,
line, message), fix, rebuild. When READY, open that version on a device and drive it:

```bash
revyl dev --platform ios --no-build --build-version-id <version-id> --no-open --detach --json --timeout 900
revyl device tap --target "Clients tab"
revyl device type --target "Name" --text "Acme"
revyl device validation "the Invoices screen shows the total 'Total: $1,240.00'"
revyl device screenshot --out shot.png
revyl device hierarchy
```
Prefer these primitives; reserve `revyl device instruction "..."` for genuinely multi-step
actions. Check several things per session — every session is a build you paid for — and
read `revyl device hierarchy` before assuming a control is where you think it is. Stop
sessions by index: `revyl device list --json` shows them, `revyl device stop -s <index>`
stops one. Never run `revyl device stop --all` — the device org is shared.

## Getting verified (the graded run)

The grader builds a **Release** simulator binary from the source you submit and runs the
four hidden tests against it on an isolated simulator. A code change reaches the grader
only through a new submission.

```bash
./submit.sh              # commits your tree, packages it, returns immediately: SUBMITTED k=<k>
                         # refused unless this exact tree has a READY development build
./submit.sh wait <k>     # polls for ≤5 min and returns; call it again until you get VERDICT
./submit.sh status       # last submission, its status, submissions used
```
A submission takes ~12–20 minutes end to end (cloud build ≈ 2–4 min + device suite
≈ 10 min). When done it prints
`VERDICT k=<k>  t_1=PASS|FAIL  t_2=…  t_3=…  final=…  regressions=[…]` and the path to
the feedback bundle `/mailbox/feedback/s<k>/`:
- `failed_criteria.json` — for each failed check: the criterion text, the judge's reasoning,
  and screenshot paths of what the device actually showed;
- `screenshots/` — those screenshots;
- `launch_errors.txt` — only if the app failed to launch or crashed.
Read the failed criteria first; diagnose against the contract; fix; verify with a
development build; resubmit. The tests themselves are never shown.

Budget: **{{MAX_SUBMISSIONS}} submissions** and **{{MAX_DEV_BUILDS}} development builds**.
A build failure on submission (`failure_kind=build_failed`) also counts as a submission —
submit only a tree whose last `./devbuild.sh wait` printed READY.

## Discipline

- Follow the contract literally: exact strings, exact numbers, exact formats.
- Checkpoint each step as you finish it: `./checkpoint.sh <n>` (see above).
- Keep the tree compiling: one broken file fails the whole build and costs a build.
- If you cannot make progress, write an honest `NOTES.md` describing what works and what
  does not — never claim a pass you did not observe in a VERDICT line.


## Evaluation at the coding deadline

At the {{WALL_CLOCK_MIN}}-minute deadline, the runner stops your agent. If you have
not submitted anything, it freezes your source, including uncommitted work, builds
it, and runs the task's required tests. Otherwise it uses your explicit submissions,
finishing any pending grade without grading your final workspace again. You cannot
edit or receive feedback after the deadline.
Use explicit submissions during the episode to get feedback while you can act on it.
